1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,486
Our review pipeline has one reviewer for everything: SQL, threads, tests. One prompt can't be an expert in all of them.

2
00:00:08,936 --> 00:00:15,191
Today, you'll add routing: a small, fast model reads the file, and sends it to the right specialist.

3
00:00:15,641 --> 00:00:18,675
And Java's compiler will make sure no case is forgotten.

4
00:00:20,225 --> 00:00:21,102
So, what is routing?

5
00:00:21,552 --> 00:00:24,701
A router agent looks at the input, and puts it in a category.

6
00:00:25,151 --> 00:00:27,488
Each category has its own specialist agent.

7
00:00:27,938 --> 00:00:32,686
Specialists get focused prompts, so each one is better at its job than a generalist.

8
00:00:33,136 --> 00:00:37,733
And only one specialist runs: the one that fits. You don't pay for the others.

9
00:00:39,183 --> 00:00:42,861
The router's job is easy: pick one of four categories.

10
00:00:43,311 --> 00:00:51,767
So it doesn't need the biggest model. Anthropic's docs describe Haiku 5.5 for classification and routing, and it's the fastest model.

11
00:00:52,217 --> 00:00:58,942
It's also much cheaper: from ten cents per million input tokens, against four dollars for Opus 5.5.

12
00:00:59,392 --> 00:01:03,481
So: a cheap model decides, and a strong model does the real work.

13
00:01:04,931 --> 00:01:05,691
When should you route?

14
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:13,381
When inputs fall into clear categories, each category needs a different prompt or tools, and classifying is easy.

15
00:01:13,831 --> 00:01:24,595
Don't route when one prompt already handles everything, or when categories overlap. And if an input needs several specialists at once, that's parallel agents: the next episode.

16
00:01:26,045 --> 00:01:27,015
Here's our use case.

17
00:01:27,465 --> 00:01:33,892
In episode two, one generalist reviewer handled every file, with one prompt, on the big model.

18
00:01:34,342 --> 00:01:42,641
Now a Haiku router reads the file first, and picks one of four specialists: security, concurrency, testing, or code quality.

19
00:01:44,091 --> 00:01:45,961
Here's the reviewer from episode two.

20
00:01:46,411 --> 00:01:53,656
One prompt for every kind of file. A SQL bug, a threading bug and a weak test all get the same generic reviewer.

21
00:01:54,106 --> 00:01:56,136
Let's give each kind of file its own expert.

22
00:01:57,586 --> 00:02:01,091
Step one: pick a model per agent. Here's our Agent class.

23
00:02:01,541 --> 00:02:04,908
I add a model field, and a second constructor that takes the model.

24
00:02:05,358 --> 00:02:10,289
The old constructor still works. It just uses Opus 5.5, like before.

25
00:02:10,739 --> 00:02:13,345
And the request uses the agent's own model.

26
00:02:14,695 --> 00:02:18,291
Step two: the router's answer. I create Route dot java.

27
00:02:18,741 --> 00:02:21,073
A route is a specialist, and a short reason.

28
00:02:23,923 --> 00:02:29,285
The specialist is an enum. So the router can only pick one of these four. It can't invent a fifth.

29
00:02:29,735 --> 00:02:34,465
And the reason is for us: we'll log it, so we can see why each file went where it went.

30
00:02:35,815 --> 00:02:39,744
Step three: the router itself, in ReviewRouter dot java.

31
00:02:40,194 --> 00:02:42,474
It's a normal agent, with three differences.

32
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,769
One: the model is Haiku 5.5. Small and fast.

33
00:02:50,219 --> 00:02:56,436
Two: the prompt defines each category in a few words. Clear categories make reliable routing.

34
00:02:56,886 --> 00:03:02,269
And three: no tools. The router only classifies. It never fixes anything.

35
00:03:03,619 --> 00:03:09,190
Step four: route to the specialists. First, a switch from each specialist to its focus.

36
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:15,971
This is a switch expression on an enum, with no default. So it must cover every value.

37
00:03:16,421 --> 00:03:21,275
If I add a fifth specialist to the enum and forget it here, the code doesn't even compile.

38
00:03:21,725 --> 00:03:27,641
Then the specialists. Each one is the same Agent class, with a focused prompt and the read file tool.

39
00:03:28,091 --> 00:03:34,008
Now main. The router gets the code directly in its task. That's one cheap call, with no tool loop.

40
00:03:37,530 --> 00:03:43,386
I print the route and its reason. Then the chosen specialist does the real review, on the big model.

41
00:03:44,736 --> 00:03:47,282
Let's test it. First, the offline check.

42
00:03:48,886 --> 00:03:53,244
Three sample files, a valid schema, and every specialist has a focus.

43
00:03:53,694 --> 00:03:57,807
Now the real thing. First file: the user service, with its SQL.

44
00:03:58,257 --> 00:04:02,229
The router picked security, and the security expert found the injection.

45
00:04:02,679 --> 00:04:06,169
Second file: the visit counter, used by many threads.

46
00:04:06,619 --> 00:04:11,384
Concurrency. It found the counter that isn't atomic, and the map that isn't thread-safe.

47
00:04:11,834 --> 00:04:12,790
And a test class.

48
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:21,901
Testing. Three files, three different experts, and each decision cost one small Haiku call.

49
00:04:23,451 --> 00:04:24,499
Four tips for your router.

50
00:04:24,949 --> 00:04:29,534
One: use a small model, and define each category in a few clear words.

51
00:04:29,984 --> 00:04:35,529
Two: return an enum, and switch on it with no default. The compiler checks every route for you.

52
00:04:35,979 --> 00:04:41,197
Three: always have a fallback category, like GENERAL, for inputs that fit nowhere.

53
00:04:41,647 --> 00:04:46,823
And four: log the router's reason. When a file goes to the wrong expert, you'll see why.

54
00:04:48,373 --> 00:04:49,027
Let's recap.

55
00:04:49,477 --> 00:04:54,090
Routing means a router classifies the input, and only the right specialist runs.

56
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:58,768
A small, cheap model makes the decision. A strong model does the work.

57
00:04:59,218 --> 00:05:04,171
And in Java, an enum with an exhaustive switch makes every route checked by the compiler.

58
00:05:05,621 --> 00:05:16,285
The full Java project is linked in the description. Next episode: parallel agents, with Java virtual threads, where several specialists review the same file at the same time.

59
00:05:16,735 --> 00:05:22,941
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI so you don't miss episode four. See you there!

